## Context

An AIDE pipeline spends most of its time in subagents the user cannot see; today the only progress signal is scrolling the terminal. The user wants a second screen that answers, from across a desk, which phase is running, on which module, by which agent, for how long, and whether it is waiting on them, and that later replays what past sessions did.

Three facts shape the design. First, the pipeline's real state already lives in the project's files: every spec, brief, plan and todo records its own status and progress, so anything a viewer derives elsewhere can only disagree with it. Second, what the files do not hold (who ran a phase, when, the prompt that started it, the order things happened) exists only as the orchestrator reports it, and those reports are best effort: an agent can forget one, a call can fail, the viewer can be closed. Third, each agent session runs its own MCP server process, several sessions can run in one project at once, and the project's repo must stay clean of anything the viewer needs.

The user's rule bounds everything: the pipeline must not depend on the viewer at all; it is only a place to see what is going on. The board adds a further constraint: it lives in a brain whose backend and layout vary, so the observer cannot learn its location by interpreting brain configuration.

## Strategy

**The agent wires the observer at boot; tools never read the instructions.** Each boot reports a session-started event carrying the project root, kanban tag and brain folder as the agent knows them, then starts the observer unless the project's instructions turn it off. One observer serves every project on a fixed address, so there is nothing to pick or record; a running one is reused and the reply links to this session's view, and a busy port is named, never swapped for another.

**Record first, then notify.** Every report is stamped with time, session and a per-session sequence number and appended to that session's own log in per-user storage outside the project first; the session's MCP server is the log's only writer. Only then, if an observer is running, the event is posted to it under a short time cap with every failure ignored. An observer that was off, slow or crashed therefore never costs an event: it replays the log later.

**Reporting is fire-and-forget, with one recovery attempt.** A report that fails, is skipped or is never made changes nothing in the pipeline. When a post does not land, the reply says the event was recorded but the observer is not running; the agent tries one start, and if that fails it tells the user once and carries on. This is how an observer crash mid-session gets noticed without the pipeline ever waiting on it.

**One intake path after a single replay.** The observer reads every project's recorded sessions once when it starts, then takes new events only through its own endpoint, which accepts posts only from the same user's tools and drops anything off-schema. Events are deduplicated on session and sequence number, so an observer started mid-session, a post overlapping the replay, or a repeated post each shows exactly once, in order. Nothing watches files, so there is no second path to race the first.

**Files decide state; events add who, when, history and what to surface.** Status is rebuilt from files, events and board on every event and page load. The tree always lists every AIDE file with its status and progress, and the user can open any of them. A waiting event names a file; the page opens it as if clicked, until the resolved event arrives or the file stops awaiting approval. A missing file surfaces nothing. A missed report costs one history row, never a stale phase or a wrong file on screen.

**The board is found from what the newest session reported.** The brain folder and kanban tag come from the newest session-started event, and a brain path the user passes overrides the folder for the project it was run from. The observer never interprets brain configuration or project instructions, because future brain backends may lay paths out differently. No usable path leaves the board unavailable with its reason; an unparseable task shows as unreadable. Neither touches the rest of the view.

**Three independent data parts, composed only by the observer, with one control.** The event log, the board reader and the status builder never depend on each other; the event log also lists every project's sessions for the home view, and the observer combines the three, so a failure in one empties only its own part. Its single control deletes one past session, removing only that session's log in per-user storage; a live session cannot be deleted and the project is never touched.

## Good examples

**Observer opened halfway through a build.** The user starts the observer after the plan was approved and two build steps checked. It replays the session log, shows the session start, the plan gate and both steps in order, then the third step arrives live. The post for step three landed during the replay; it appears once.

**A forgotten report.** The orchestrator never reports that the plan was approved. The plan file now reads approved, so status shows Build with the step count from the file and no waiting gate. History simply has no approval row; nothing is guessed in its place.

**Port taken by another program.** At boot the observer's fixed port is held by another program. The start tool declines and names the port; the agent tells the user once and the cycle runs on, every event still landing in the session log for a later replay.

## Bad examples

**The waiting card trusts the event log.** A gate-waiting event was recorded, the user approved the plan by editing the file, and the resolved report was skipped. The page still shouts "waiting on you" because it read the gate from events instead of confirming it against the file's status.

**A slow observer stalls a subagent launch.** Recording waits on the post without a cap, or treats a refused connection as an error the agent must handle. The pipeline now runs slower or differently whenever the observer is down, which the user said must never happen.

**A guessed board.** No session has reported a brain folder yet, so the observer reads the brain config or falls back to a default folder and shows another project's tasks as if they were this one's, instead of saying the board is unavailable and why.

## References

- User decision on one observer per machine, relayed by the orchestrator (2026-11-23) -- boot with no port to pick or record, a running observer reused with the link to the calling session's view, and the event log listing every project's sessions for the home view
- Pipeline flow and edge-case behavior settled with the user, relayed by the orchestrator (2026-11-22) -- every Strategy decision: session-started report before start, record-then-notify, one-restart recovery, replay-plus-endpoint intake, files-decide-state, board location, the single delete control
- `projects/aidemd/pipeline-viewer/handoff.md` -- the glanceability requirement and the questions the second screen must answer; its in-project event log and file-watcher intake were superseded by the user's 2026-11-22 decisions
- User decision on surfacing files, relayed by the orchestrator (2026-11-22) -- decision 5: the full tree of AIDE files always shows, a waiting event opens its named file as if clicked, and a missing file surfaces nothing
